-To roll back, deploy the previous release without migrating: `scripts/deploy_secure_backend.sh rollback <git-ref>`, then the matching web gateway. Do not use the platform's plain "redeploy previous", because that image runs `migrate` at start and exits with `Can't locate revision`. Migrations 0007 and 0008 are additive and the previous API runs on them. A restored backup is the only way back to an older schema.
+To roll back, deploy the previous release without migrating: `scripts/deploy_secure_backend.sh rollback <git-ref>`, then the matching web gateway. Do not use the platform's plain "redeploy previous", because that image runs `migrate` at start and exits with `Can't locate revision`. Migrations 0007 to 0010 are additive and the previous API runs on them. Two things change when the API is rolled back below 0009/0010: roll the camera workers back with it, because an older API answers `422` to the newer workers' heartbeats and managed cameras go stale; and an older API does not know legal holds, so it purges or expires held evidence and export packages as if no hold existed. A restored backup is the only way back to an older schema.
+
+### Camera failure notifications are not active in production
+The API records a failure notice in the camera history and the audit log, but nobody receives a push: no VAPID keys are set, the police UI has no push subscription, and nothing schedules `python -m app.cli notify-camera-failures --tenant <slug>` (the only way a silent worker is noticed). Until those three exist, watch the camera panel.
 
 ### Four-eyes actions need two administrators
 Config approval, re-enabling a camera and export approval all refuse a single person. Create a second MFA-enrolled administrator before using them, and do not rerun `demo-accounts` or `scripts/deploy_demo.sh` afterwards. With one administrator these actions stay refused: that is the intended fail-closed behaviour.
